            ⛰️ Moderate Hikes
          </a>
          <a href="#daytrips" class="px-4 py-2 bg-cyan-50 text-cyan-700 rounded-lg hover:bg-cyan-100 transition-colors font-medium text-sm">
            🚗 Farther Day Trips
          </a>
          <a href="#all" class="px-4 py-2 bg-purple-50 text-purple-700 rounded-lg hover:bg-purple-100 transition-colors font-medium text-sm">
            📍 All {nearbyHikes.length} Trails
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Intro Content -->
      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-16 bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 shadow-sm border border-stone-200">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-stone-900 mb-4">Providence Doesn't Have Mountains — It Has Something Better: Options</h2>

        <p class="text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
          Rhode Island's highest point, Jerimoth Hill, tops out at 840 feet — so if you're searching for
          alpine summits, Providence isn't the place to start looking locally. What it does have is a tight
          cluster of easy, close-in hills perfect for a morning walk, plus fast highway access to real
          elevation once you're willing to drive: Connecticut's ridgelines to the west, southeastern
          Massachusetts woodland to the east, and Mount Monadnock in New Hampshire about two hours north.
        </p>

        <p class="text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
          This guide covers all {nearbyHikes.length} trails within 100 miles of Providence, sorted by actual
          distance from real trailhead coordinates — not the town center. Each trail includes:
        </p>

