            <div class="p-4 text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
              Rhode Island's own trail cluster — including Snake Den, Bare Rock, Cat Rocks and Round Rocks — offers short, easy hikes
              within 10 miles of downtown Providence. All are well suited to beginners and families.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="group">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 bg-stone-50 rounded-xl hover:bg-stone-100 transition-colors font-bold text-stone-900 list-none">
              <span>Do I need a parking pass for hiking trails near Providence?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 transform transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/>
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="p-4 text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
              Most of Rhode Island's state management areas and Connecticut's blue-blazed trails have free trailhead parking, though
              some fill early on weekends. State parks farther out — including Monadnock State Park in New Hampshire — do charge a
              day-use parking fee. Check each trail's parking information page for specifics.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="group">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 bg-stone-50 rounded-xl hover:bg-stone-100 transition-colors font-bold text-stone-900 list-none">
